<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sino-British Joint Declaration</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1984">1984</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sino-British Joint Declaration was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 19 December 1984 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> and Zhao Ziyang. Prompted by the expiry of the New Territories lease in 1997, it provided for the transfer of all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> on 1 July 1997, with its capitalist system and way of life to remain unchanged for fifty years under 'one country, two systems'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sino-british-joint-declaration.jpg" alt="Sino-British Joint Declaration" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomoaki INABA from Brussels, Belgium · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Hall_of_the_People_%E4%BA%BA%E6%B0%91%E5%A4%A7%E4%BC%9A%E5%A0%82_(Tomoaki_INABA)_-_Flickr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> became a British colony in 1842 after the British defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in the First Opium War, and in 1860 the Kowloon Peninsula and Stonecutters Island were added after the Second Opium War. In 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> obtained from the Qing a 99-year lease of the New Territories, rent-free, as a compensatory concession after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> obtained a lease at Guangzhouwan. The lease's end in 1997 set the deadline. In March 1979 Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murray-maclehose">Murray MacLehose</a> raised the issue with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>, who said that Hong Kong held a special status that would continue to be respected.</p><p>Formal negotiations began in September 1982, when Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> and Governor Edward Youde went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to meet Premier Zhao Ziyang and Deng. The first phase turned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> said it would resume sovereignty and administration in 1997, by force if necessary, and proposed a special administrative region run by local residents with existing systems kept for 50 years. Talks stalled until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> conceded sovereignty over the entire territory, and a British attempt to extend its administration was rejected. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> dollar fell sharply on Black Saturday in September 1983, the British accepted in November that they would hold no authority after 1997.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>A draft was initialled on 26 September 1984, and Thatcher and Zhao signed the treaty in the Great Hall of the People on 19 December 1984. Ratifications were exchanged on 27 May 1985, and both governments registered it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> on 12 June 1985. In the main text <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> declared it would resume <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> on 1 July 1997, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> agreed to transfer it. A special administrative region would be autonomous in local affairs, and its economic, social and legal systems would remain basically unchanged.</p><p>Annex I listed 14 Chinese policies: a high degree of autonomy except in defence and diplomatic affairs, and the capitalist economy and civil liberties unchanged for 50 years, to be set out in the Basic Law. Annex II created the Joint Liaison Group, an advisory body, which ended in 2000. Annex III protected land leases, allowing extension to no later than 30 June 2047. Memoranda created a new nationality, British National (Overseas), without right of abode in the United Kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>From 2014 the Chinese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> governments stated that the Declaration had no continuing legal effect, and in 2017 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> called it a historical document. The United Kingdom and the G7 maintained that it remained valid. After the national security legislation of 2020 and the election changes of 2021 the United Kingdom declared China in a state of ongoing non-compliance, and China rejected such accusations as foreign interference.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sino-British Joint Declaration” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Because the New Territories lease expired in 1997, Britain and China signed the <b>Joint Declaration</b> in 1984.</blockquote><a href="/state/hong-kong-british">Hong Kong (British)</a></li></ul></section>
